WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:02.025
- His Four Hour Workweek book was on the

00:00:02.025 --> 00:00:04.897
best-seller list for
seven consecutive years.

00:00:04.897 --> 00:00:07.073
His next books The Four Hour Body and

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.889
The Four Hour Chef became
instant best-sellers.

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:13.945
He's an angel investor and or
advisor to Facebook, Twitter,

00:00:13.945 --> 00:00:18.026
StumbleUpon, Evernote, and
Uber, among other companies.

00:00:18.026 --> 00:00:22.193
He's Tim Ferriss, and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:27.035
- I diversified identity, in some fashion.

00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:29.023
I think this is important,
so that your self-worth

00:00:29.023 --> 00:00:31.738
isn't only associated with your start up.

00:00:31.738 --> 00:00:33.512
That's probably a
controversial recommendation.

00:00:33.512 --> 00:00:37.326
What that means is you
should actually spend time

00:00:37.326 --> 00:00:38.639
involved with other skills.

00:00:38.639 --> 00:00:40.922
In San Francisco, for instance,
many start up founders

00:00:40.922 --> 00:00:43.235
I know who are very
efficient and effective have

00:00:43.235 --> 00:00:46.570
their start up, rock
climbing, they get outside.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.625
They do a few things so
that if one goes well,

00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:52.169
they can remain optimistic each week.

00:00:52.169 --> 00:00:53.865
Which is very difficult if you're only

00:00:53.865 --> 00:00:55.823
directed at your start up.

00:00:55.823 --> 00:00:58.217
I only invest in things that I understand

00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:00.783
and things that I use or would use myself.

00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:03.211
This is really really really important.

00:01:03.211 --> 00:01:05.932
Anytime I step outside
of that, is usually when

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.877
the universe kicks me
in the nuts really hard,

00:01:07.877 --> 00:01:09.170
and I lose my money.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.767
I think it's critical
to, as an entrepreneur,

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.214
scratch your own itch.

00:01:13.214 --> 00:01:15.076
It increases the odds of success or

00:01:15.076 --> 00:01:17.076
the probability of success.

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.071
And as an investor, I
look for people who are

00:01:19.071 --> 00:01:20.467
scratching their own itch.

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:22.530
When you're thinking
about a product launch,

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.790
the first thing I would ask is,

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.805
is it the right time to
be product launching?

00:01:25.805 --> 00:01:27.965
So if your product isn't figured out,

00:01:27.965 --> 00:01:30.819
if you can't fill the orders,

00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:33.279
now is not the right time
to be in the Techcrunch,

00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:36.510
in my opinion, or in any
other major media outlet.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.218
Once you're in a major media outlet,

00:01:38.218 --> 00:01:40.196
they're not going to cover you again,

00:01:40.196 --> 00:01:42.493
for some period of time or ever.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:44.042
If you're in the business
section of The New York Times,

00:01:44.042 --> 00:01:45.570
they're not going to
cover you four weeks later

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.489
when your product is
better and you're ready.

00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:50.947
So make sure that you pick your timing.

00:01:50.947 --> 00:01:54.403
A few things, there's paid
exposure and unpaid exposure.

00:01:54.403 --> 00:01:58.367
I like to focus on unpaid
because it makes you,

00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:02.223
it forces you to make better
decisions in the beginning.

00:02:02.223 --> 00:02:05.197
So I would read, as I always say,

00:02:05.197 --> 00:02:07.415
The 22 Immutable Laws of Marketing,

00:02:07.415 --> 00:02:09.732
1000 True Fans written by Kevin Kelly,

00:02:09.732 --> 00:02:12.855
I would also read Bestselling
Book PR and Publicity,

00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:17.204
I think it is, by Rick
Frishman F-R-I-S-H-M-A-N.

00:02:17.204 --> 00:02:20.073
All of the techniques
apply to more than books.

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.128
And learn to tell the story.

00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:23.685
What are you focusing on?

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.572
Are you using yourself to tell the story,

00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:27.690
and then the product is associated.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.243
Is it a trend piece?

00:02:29.243 --> 00:02:32.152
Are you trying to tie what
you're doing into a greater trend

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.237
which is a very effective
way of getting media.

00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:35.823
In which case, you're going
to need other examples.

00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:39.014
You need to get comfortable
bringing in, sort of,

00:02:39.014 --> 00:02:43.765
cooperative competitors to
create that trend piece.

00:02:43.765 --> 00:02:46.895
Learn the art of the
pitch and of messaging.

00:02:46.895 --> 00:02:49.148
Only once you have figured
that out, would I say,

00:02:49.148 --> 00:02:51.318
then you go out and you
pay to acquire people,

00:02:51.318 --> 00:02:53.431
because now if your messaging is off,

00:02:53.431 --> 00:02:55.206
it's costing you money.

00:02:55.206 --> 00:02:57.914
You need to know what
your unique strengths are

00:02:57.914 --> 00:02:59.584
and be willing to delegate.

00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:01.628
So I would say that,
the question of whether

00:03:01.628 --> 00:03:04.366
you should take a skill,
learn it yourself,

00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:07.992
delegate it to someone else,
or hire a new team member,

00:03:07.992 --> 00:03:12.159
are determined by having a
very accurate self-assessment,

00:03:13.535 --> 00:03:15.526
an assessment of your team's skills.

00:03:15.526 --> 00:03:18.428
Not only what you can do,
but what you should be doing.

00:03:18.428 --> 00:03:19.760
That's very important.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.286
So if you're a start up
CEO or founder and you have

00:03:22.286 --> 00:03:27.186
a team of say, two to 10
people, as soon as you have

00:03:27.186 --> 00:03:31.131
funds to hire, you should not be focused

00:03:31.131 --> 00:03:33.708
on the things that you are bad at.

00:03:33.708 --> 00:03:36.968
Sounds very very intuitive
but it's not because

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:38.739
you might have enough
time to do those things,

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:40.411
but the question is, do
you have the attention

00:03:40.411 --> 00:03:43.969
to dedicate to those things so
they're not an interruption.

00:03:43.969 --> 00:03:46.270
Especially if you're a
venture-backed start up,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:47.554
and you're competing
against other companies

00:03:47.554 --> 00:03:49.682
that also have a lot of funding,

00:03:49.682 --> 00:03:52.155
the game and the race goes to the swift

00:03:52.155 --> 00:03:54.954
so you have to have a lot of velocity.

00:03:54.954 --> 00:03:57.795
You need to be able to sell,
whether it's Kickstarter,

00:03:57.795 --> 00:03:59.387
whether it's New York Times,

00:03:59.387 --> 00:04:01.185
whether it's your local newspaper,

00:04:01.185 --> 00:04:03.841
you need to be able to
communicate effectively and sell.

00:04:03.841 --> 00:04:07.067
Which is why, when entrepreneurs
as me for books to read

00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:08.491
outside of business books,

00:04:08.491 --> 00:04:11.491
I say On Writing Well, Bird by Bird,

00:04:12.725 --> 00:04:16.558
all books on writing,
improving communication.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.520
David Ogilvy, Ogilvy on Advertising.

00:04:21.377 --> 00:04:25.258
Study print ads, where you
have very little space.

00:04:25.258 --> 00:04:27.163
I think those are all very very effective.

00:04:27.163 --> 00:04:29.139
The best entrepreneurs I've ever met

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:32.171
are all good communicators.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:36.218
It's the one, perhaps one of
the very few unifying factors.

00:04:36.218 --> 00:04:38.126
They're exceptional communicators.

00:04:38.126 --> 00:04:41.564
Practice is the only way to
get better at communicating.

00:04:41.564 --> 00:04:43.995
And practicing speaking
does not provide you,

00:04:43.995 --> 00:04:46.050
generally, with something to review later.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.659
Which is why the written
word is so powerful.

00:04:48.659 --> 00:04:51.262
Writing is thought crystallized
on a piece of paper

00:04:51.262 --> 00:04:53.435
that can then be reviewed.

00:04:53.435 --> 00:04:54.805
It's very difficult to
do that with speech,

00:04:54.805 --> 00:04:57.114
which is why I recommend people focus on

00:04:57.114 --> 00:04:59.503
honing their skills in writing.

00:04:59.503 --> 00:05:03.356
And take time to study
good direct response ads.

00:05:03.356 --> 00:05:07.035
Take time to, for instance,
maybe develop a swipe file.

00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:09.509
So one of the things
that I did for years is,

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:13.443
anytime I bought something,
I would identify the ad,

00:05:13.443 --> 00:05:17.453
the phone call, the print ad, the offer,

00:05:17.453 --> 00:05:20.593
whatever it was that
made me decide to buy it,

00:05:20.593 --> 00:05:22.484
and I would either cut
it out, or record it,

00:05:22.484 --> 00:05:25.134
put it into Evernote, whatever
it might be, and save it.

00:05:25.134 --> 00:05:27.324
So I had a record of
everything that I bought

00:05:27.324 --> 00:05:29.279
and why I bought it.

00:05:29.279 --> 00:05:31.302
And then I could go back and
review it and I'd be like,

00:05:31.302 --> 00:05:34.259
"Oh, looks like I fell
for this this and this,"

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:37.242
not in a bad way, but it looks
like this tipped me from,

00:05:37.242 --> 00:05:41.215
eh, or not interested, to yeah I'll spend

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:44.215
five, 50, 500, 5000 dollars on that.

00:05:45.107 --> 00:05:46.618
I save all that.

00:05:46.618 --> 00:05:50.091
Everything that works in
sales has been done already.

00:05:50.091 --> 00:05:52.078
You do not need to reinvent the wheel.

00:05:52.078 --> 00:05:54.324
So just keep track of
the crap that you buy,

00:05:54.324 --> 00:05:56.286
or the awesome stuff that you buy,

00:05:56.286 --> 00:05:58.361
and decide what was the trigger,

00:05:58.361 --> 00:05:59.571
what pushed me over the edge?

00:05:59.571 --> 00:06:00.999
And then just sell to people like you.

00:06:00.999 --> 00:06:01.832
That's what I do.

00:06:01.832 --> 00:06:02.748
It's really easy.

00:06:02.748 --> 00:06:03.581
People are like, "Who's your market?"

00:06:03.581 --> 00:06:07.660
I'm like, "Well, you know,
I'm a 30-something SF based

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.115
tech-savvy male, so why don't
I just sell to those people?"

00:06:10.115 --> 00:06:13.187
It makes it a lot easier
than trying to guess.

00:06:13.187 --> 00:06:14.517
Ideas are worth nothing.

00:06:14.517 --> 00:06:17.865
I mean ideas are really,
they're not a dime a dozen.

00:06:17.865 --> 00:06:19.337
They're just nothing.

00:06:19.337 --> 00:06:22.211
All the good founders I
know, even the bad founders,

00:06:22.211 --> 00:06:23.761
can come up with ideas all day long.

00:06:23.761 --> 00:06:25.417
It doesn't mean anything.

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:27.680
You have to be able to execute,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.831
and one of the, sort of,

00:06:29.831 --> 00:06:32.106
maxims that I was told once was,

00:06:32.106 --> 00:06:33.977
"Your success will be
measured in the number of

00:06:33.977 --> 00:06:36.476
uncomfortable conversations
you're willing to have."

00:06:36.476 --> 00:06:39.240
So I think of it as, if an
entrepreneur should, say,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.714
start a business based on
one of their passions or

00:06:42.714 --> 00:06:45.864
if they should find the most
attractive market opportunity

00:06:45.864 --> 00:06:47.531
and build a business out of that,

00:06:47.531 --> 00:06:49.123
whether they like it or not.

00:06:49.123 --> 00:06:51.147
I don't think it's either
or, I think you need both.

00:06:51.147 --> 00:06:53.504
I would say, look at your
credit card statement.

00:06:53.504 --> 00:06:55.762
Identify where you spend an unusual

00:06:55.762 --> 00:06:56.903
percentage of your income.

00:06:56.903 --> 00:06:58.775
Were you price-insensitive?

00:06:58.775 --> 00:07:01.160
Then how old are you, what gender are you,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.420
where do you live, and design a product

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.652
that has all of those things intersected.

00:07:07.652 --> 00:07:12.205
It's very easy to be seduced
by doing things efficiently,

00:07:12.205 --> 00:07:14.722
trying to do a lot of things well.

00:07:14.722 --> 00:07:16.506
I don't believe that's
the most important thing.

00:07:16.506 --> 00:07:17.647
The most important thing is picking

00:07:17.647 --> 00:07:20.212
the right things to do in the first place.

00:07:20.212 --> 00:07:23.051
And that's being effective
versus just being efficient.

00:07:23.051 --> 00:07:25.456
So within a start up,
it's, success I don't think

00:07:25.456 --> 00:07:28.825
is measured by how many
things you can do at once.

00:07:28.825 --> 00:07:31.463
It's measured by how
effectively you're able to pick

00:07:31.463 --> 00:07:34.343
one or two things that
are the highest priority.

00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:37.478
And there's always going
to be a seduction to

00:07:37.478 --> 00:07:40.144
get distracted and focus
on 20 things at once.

00:07:40.144 --> 00:07:43.102
And the most effective entrepreneurs
I know are very good at

00:07:43.102 --> 00:07:44.806
focusing on one or two things

00:07:44.806 --> 00:07:46.130
and either eliminating the rest

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:47.373
or delegating to other people

00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:49.294
or outsourcing it so it's
not done within the team

00:07:49.294 --> 00:07:51.348
but it's actually to third parties.

00:07:51.348 --> 00:07:55.515
First-time entrepreneurs tend
to miscalculate is everything.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.313
What I mean by that is

00:08:00.375 --> 00:08:03.470
optimism has a place but I think,

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.260
even more so for the
first-time entrepreneur,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.895
you need to be pragmatically pessimistic.

00:08:08.895 --> 00:08:12.532
What I mean by that is you need
to define all the worst-case

00:08:12.532 --> 00:08:17.283
scenarios in terms of financial
loss, time loss, et cetera.

00:08:17.283 --> 00:08:20.456
Look at what you will
learn if that happens and

00:08:20.456 --> 00:08:24.679
accept and come to terms with
that before you ever start.

00:08:24.679 --> 00:08:28.310
If you don't do that,
and you go straight into

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.923
battling the world, trying
to conquer the world

00:08:30.923 --> 00:08:32.571
with rose-colored glasses on,

00:08:32.571 --> 00:08:34.337
the first time you hit a major hiccup,

00:08:34.337 --> 00:08:37.488
you're going to become really
demoralized and you will quit.

00:08:37.488 --> 00:08:41.189
So optimism, all that rah
rah rah, that's awesome,

00:08:41.189 --> 00:08:43.917
but you need to hope for the best

00:08:43.917 --> 00:08:47.150
and prepare for the worst
before you ever get started.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.983
I always recommend that.

00:08:48.983 --> 00:08:50.696
I think it's important to
have at least one or two

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.828
numbers that you track on a weekly basis

00:08:52.828 --> 00:08:55.628
so that could be for web-based properties,

00:08:55.628 --> 00:08:57.240
or services, or products,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.837
it could be weekly average uniques.

00:09:00.837 --> 00:09:03.924
What it is doesn't
matter that much, uh but,

00:09:03.924 --> 00:09:06.289
matters less than having a metric

00:09:06.289 --> 00:09:08.855
that you try to improve every week.

00:09:08.855 --> 00:09:09.829
You can have one or two of them,

00:09:09.829 --> 00:09:11.288
you really shouldn't
have many more than that,

00:09:11.288 --> 00:09:12.893
I don't think.

00:09:12.893 --> 00:09:16.365
Could be conversion from
sign up to, let's say,

00:09:16.365 --> 00:09:18.706
first stage of sign up
to completion of sign up.

00:09:18.706 --> 00:09:20.730
There are many different
metrics that you can use,

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:23.306
but I think that to keep the team focused,

00:09:23.306 --> 00:09:25.001
especially in a small company,

00:09:25.001 --> 00:09:26.043
it's very important to have one or two

00:09:26.043 --> 00:09:28.493
that you focus on a weekly basis.

00:09:28.493 --> 00:09:30.419
I think failure would be a big piece,

00:09:30.419 --> 00:09:32.899
even though I view
failure differently than,

00:09:32.899 --> 00:09:33.931
I think, many people.

00:09:33.931 --> 00:09:36.185
I view it as a feedback
mechanism but if you look at,

00:09:36.185 --> 00:09:38.815
even, let's say The Four Hour Workweek,

00:09:38.815 --> 00:09:40.506
didn't start off called
The Four Hour Workweek.

00:09:40.506 --> 00:09:43.343
It was also turned down by
26 out of 27 publishers.

00:09:43.343 --> 00:09:45.796
And hopefully pretty soon
it will be celebrating

00:09:45.796 --> 00:09:49.287
four years unbroken on the New
York Times best-seller list.

00:09:49.287 --> 00:09:52.147
And I like to, whenever possible,

00:09:52.147 --> 00:09:54.536
illustrate those failures
because people will,

00:09:54.536 --> 00:09:58.120
everyone will face failure or mistakes.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.399
And those are the
divergent points from which

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:03.338
they can either go in a good
direction or a bad direction.

00:10:03.338 --> 00:10:05.813
And I want to illustrate that oftentimes

00:10:05.813 --> 00:10:08.464
the finish line is only 100
feet from where you stand,

00:10:08.464 --> 00:10:10.839
but it might seem that
it's 100 miles away.

00:10:10.839 --> 00:10:11.831
- Thank you guys for watching.

00:10:11.831 --> 00:10:15.911
I made this video because
Chris7820 asked me to,

00:10:15.911 --> 00:10:17.576
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:10:17.576 --> 00:10:19.234
that you want me to profile next,

00:10:19.234 --> 00:10:21.963
leave it in the comments below,
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:21.963 --> 00:10:23.374
I'd also love to know what you think of

00:10:23.374 --> 00:10:26.259
Tim Ferriss as an entrepreneur
and what your favorite

00:10:26.259 --> 00:10:28.224
Tim Ferriss book is.

00:10:28.224 --> 00:10:29.596
Mine is The Four Hour Workweek.

00:10:29.596 --> 00:10:30.740
What's yours?

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:31.990
Leave it below.

